GXM-1 (2026-09-14) - `Application::frame()` must OPEN the frame.

File: library/lib/core/application.cpp, in `Application::frame()`.

WHAT WAS WRONG. The function called `clear()` then `nvgBeginFrame()`, drew,
called `nvgEndFrame()` and `videoContext->endFrame()` - but never
`videoContext->beginFrame()`. On PS Vita that is the call carrying
`sceGxmBeginScene` (see platforms/psv/psv_video.cpp), so no scene was ever
opened: every nanovg draw went to a context with nowhere to put it,
`sceGxmEndScene` answered SCE_GXM_ERROR_NOT_WITHIN_SCENE exactly once, and the
system killed the application about two seconds into the main loop.

HOW IT WAS PROVEN. A frame counter placed inside `gxmBeginFrameEx` stayed at
ZERO for the whole life of the process while `gxmEndFrame` ran. The core dump
puts the main thread inside `gxmnvg__renderFlush` - the flush of a frame that
was never begun.

WHY NOTHING ELSE NOTICED. On the desktop backends `beginFrame()` is nearly a
no-op (GLFW and SDL only forward it to D3D11) and on Switch it is cheap. The PS
Vita is the one platform where that call is the frame.

Put this back, immediately above the `clear()` call:

    // Begin frame and clear
    /* LOCAL FIX halyard 2026-09-14 (GXM-1) - THE FRAME WAS NEVER OPENED.
     * ... (keep the full comment from the patch) ... */
    videoContext->beginFrame();
    videoContext->clear(Application::getTheme().getColor("brls/clear"));
